{#
    A frame, and — where it is a specimen — the caption that says what it is.

    Two nodes share this template because they are the same thing: a document
    of somebody else's, shown inside this one. A video has a title and nothing
    else to say. A specimen names the card and the size of its measurement,
    because `make fit` checks that number. A reader who wants to know what
    they look at must not have to open the source.

    **The element, and nothing but the address of it.** What the two need is a
    frame with the size, the border and the caption every other block on the
    page has. `sds-embed` is where that decision happens once. See its own
    file for the two shapes and why an embed has to state which one it is.
    Left to the renderer this was a raw frame with the browser's 1996 ridge
    around it, as wide as its `width` attribute said. It ran straight out of
    any column narrower than that.

    The `<iframe>` is a property, like everything else. `make guides` renders
    each element before the page goes out — `scripts/lib/prerender.ts` — so a
    reader with no JavaScript has the frame, and this file draws none.

    Not lazy, and that is the element's decision rather than this file's. A
    specimen is the evidence on the page. A frame that loads on scroll is a
    frame that is blank in every screenshot the gate takes. That is the one
    place somebody looks at all of them at once.
#}
{%- if node.hasOption('specimen') -%}
{#- Fixed. The card declares the viewport of its measurement and this is that
    number. So the frame keeps it and scrolls where the column is narrower. A
    card reflowed to fit documents a layout nothing checked.

    The caption is a sentence and arrives as one. What a caption says belongs
    to the document, and how it sets belongs to the component. -#}
<sds-embed src="{{ asset(node.url) }}"
    label="{{ node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }}"
    caption="{{ node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }} · {{ node.option('viewport') }}"
    {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-embed>
{%- else -%}
{#- Fluid. A player's `width` and `height` are its native size and not the
    size it wants. It fills the column it gets and holds the shape those two
    state, which is the one thing about it that must not change. -#}
<sds-embed src="{{ node.url }}"
    ratio="{{ node.option('width')|default(560) }} / {{ node.option('height')|default(315) }}"
    {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('title') %} label="{{ node.option('title') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('allow') %} allow="{{ node.option('allow') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('allowfullscreen') and node.option('allowfullscreen') %} allowfullscreen{% endif -%}
></sds-embed>
{%- endif -%}
